Make RECT_T the same layout in libawt and libawt_xawt.

RECT_T is ABI, not a local convenience. Region.c and rect.c live in libawt and
write RECT_T records into a caller-supplied buffer; XRBackendNative.c and
X11SurfaceData.c live in libawt_xawt and declare that buffer as XRectangle[].

libawt is compiled -DMACOSX because iOS is in the macOS platform family, while
patch 0004 compiles the XAWT sources -UMACOSX so they see the unix X11
declarations. The int-based layout in the MACOSX branch therefore gave the two
libraries different strides: libawt wrote 16-byte {int x,y,width,height}
records into an 8-byte XRectangle {short x,y,width,height} array.

Every clip handed to XRenderSetPictureClipRectangles arrived as [0,0 0x0] --
the first record's int x filled XRectangle.x and .y, and its int y filled
.width and .height. A zero-area clip silently discards all rendering, with no X
protocol error, so every XRender surface rendered black: VolatileImage fills,
blits, Swing button gradients and on-screen window contents alike. Only the
OpenGL pipeline, which does not go through this path, painted correctly.

Fixed by giving the MACOSX branch XRectangle's field widths rather than by
selecting XRectangle itself. Including <X11/Xlib.h> here instead would drag
Xlib into every libawt translation unit that includes Region.h, which regressed
the window-surface path with "native ops missing" on the toolkit thread.

diff --git a/src/java.desktop/unix/native/common/awt/utility/rect.h b/src/java.desktop/unix/native/common/awt/utility/rect.h
index ceea38f434..79f7997e05 100644
--- a/src/java.desktop/unix/native/common/awt/utility/rect.h
+++ b/src/java.desktop/unix/native/common/awt/utility/rect.h
@@ -32,12 +32,25 @@
 #include <X11/Xlib.h>
 typedef XRectangle RECT_T;
 #else
-// OSX still needs this for BitmapToYXBandedRectangles
+/*
+ * OSX still needs this for BitmapToYXBandedRectangles.
+ *
+ * The field widths are ABI, not a local convenience. Region.c and rect.c live
+ * in libawt, which is compiled -DMACOSX because iOS is in the macOS platform
+ * family; they write RECT_T records into buffers that the XAWT sources declare
+ * as XRectangle[] and compile -UMACOSX (see the XAWT-as-unix patch). An int
+ * layout here gave the two libraries different strides -- 16-byte records
+ * written into an 8-byte array -- so every clip arrived as [0,0 0x0], which
+ * silently discarded all XRender output and rendered every accelerated surface
+ * black. Matching XRectangle's short fields keeps both sides in step without
+ * pulling <X11/Xlib.h> into every libawt translation unit that includes
+ * Region.h.
+ */
 typedef struct {
-    int x;
-    int y;
-    int width;
-    int height;
+    short x;
+    short y;
+    unsigned short width;
+    unsigned short height;
 } RECT_T;
 #endif /* !MACOSX */
 
